course: language
lesson: 55
title: ★★★ Olympiad: hyperbole and downplaying puzzles
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This lesson closes the block with puzzles. Each one hides a trap: a real number next to a fake one, a word that is hyperbole in one place and plain truth in another, two kinds of talk stacked on top of each other, or a dialect word that sounds like something else.
explain: Picture a maths problem that begins «Gran has told me a thousand times she was born in 1950». One sentence, two numbers: 1950 is a fact, the thousand is a moan. The olympiad pupil keeps the fact and throws away the moan.
explain: Sometimes the layers stack: Dad watching a hurricane on the news and saying, deadpan, «Hmm, a touch of drizzle out there» is understatement used as a joke — and the pictures show the truth.
explain: The tools are the ones you already have: compare the words with the evidence; ask whether it is bigger, smaller or the opposite; keep the last corrected number; and when someone plays down real trouble, check and tell.
fact: In one sentence one number can be real and another fake: «Gran has told me a thousand times she was born in 1950» gives a real year (1950) and a hyperbolic count (a thousand), so in 2000 she was 50.
fact: Understatement can be a joke: «a touch of drizzle» said deadpan about a hurricane is sarcastic understatement; «deadpan» means with a straight, serious face.
fact: «It's not that I don't like it, pet…» usually comes before a «but» and means the speaker doesn't really like it; undoing the two «nots» here gives the wrong answer.
fact: «Over 1 million people visited the museum this year» is a measured number tied to a place and a time; «A MILLION reasons to love summer!» is a feeling dressed up as a number.
fact: The same word can be hyperbole or truth: «starving» after school lunch is hyperbole, but in a history book about a famine, where people had no food for weeks, it is literal.
fact: In Yorkshire, «nesh» means someone who feels the cold easily, and «nobbut a bit fresh» means «only slightly cool»; a grandad saying «you're nesh, you» is teasing.
fact: «I did like a hundred keepy-uppies, well, 23» — the last, corrected number counts: 23.
fact: «I may have slightly broken the window» about a smashed window is downplaying to avoid trouble; the honest «I broke the window» is better.
fact: Playing down your own gift, like «Oh, it's nothing much», is polite in many cultures; a big thank you is still the right reply.
fact: «Could be worse» after losing 6–0 is downplaying to cope; it went badly, and «next time» is a kind reply.
fact: In Singapore English, «wah» shows surprise, «damn long» means very long, «lah» adds feeling, and «confirm» means «definitely»; but «confirm one hour» is still a guess, not a check.
fact: «Not bad for a beginner» is praise with a limit: it is good, and there is still more to learn.
exercise: «Gran has told me a thousand times she was born in 1950. How old was she in 2000?» | 50 | 1000 | 1050 | 950
exercise: ★★★ In Gran's problem, which numbers should you use? | 1950 and 2000 | 1000 and 2000 | 1000 and 1950 | All three numbers
exercise: A hurricane on the news, trees flying. Dad, deadpan: «Hmm, a touch of drizzle out there». What is he doing? | Joking by playing a huge storm down | Telling you it is only drizzling | Saying the hurricane is far away | Exaggerating how big the storm really is
exercise: Gran tastes Ella's spicy curry: «It's not that I don't like it, pet…». What does she probably mean? | She doesn't really like it, but is being kind | She likes it very much, because two nots cancel | She wants a second bowl of the same curry | She doesn't know what curry tastes like
exercise: Headline A: «Over 1 million people visited the museum this year». Headline B: «A MILLION reasons to love summer!». Which is a measured number? | A | B | Both | Neither
exercise: A history book says «The people were starving» about a famine with no food for weeks. What is true? | It is literal: people were really dying of hunger | It is hyperbole, like a child home from school | It is understatement, because it was worse | It is sarcasm about the people who lived through the famine
exercise: A Yorkshire grandad: «Eh, you're nesh, you. It's nobbut a bit fresh». What does «nesh» mean? | Someone who feels the cold easily | Someone who is very brave and tough | Someone who is wearing new clothes | Someone who has a cold in the nose
exercise: What does the grandad think about the weather? | It's only slightly cool | It's freezing cold | It's boiling hot | It's going to snow heavily soon
exercise: Jamal: «I did like a hundred keepy-uppies, well, 23, but that's still a record». What is his record? | 23 | 100 | 123 | 77
exercise: Max, after a crash while playing football indoors: «I may have slightly broken the window». What is the window really like? | Smashed | A bit scratched | Just fine | Slightly cracked at one corner
exercise: What would be the better thing for Max to say? | «I broke the window, sorry» | «The ball broke it, not me» | «It was already broken» | «It's only slightly broken»
exercise: Mrs Tanaka gives Lucy a beautiful, expensive scarf: «Oh, it's nothing much». What should Lucy say? | A big thank you | «Okay, never mind» | «Why, is it cheap?» | Nothing at all
exercise: After losing 6–0 a player says «Could be worse». How did the match go? | Badly, and he's trying to cope | Quite well, it was nearly a draw | Brilliantly, a big win | It was cancelled
exercise: In a Singapore food court: «Wah, the queue damn long lah, confirm one hour». What does «confirm» mean here? | Definitely | Checked on a clock | Maybe | Booked a place
exercise: Does «confirm one hour» mean the teenager has checked the time? | No, it's still his guess | Yes, he timed the queue | Yes, a sign at the front told him | No, he means one minute
exercise: The swimming teacher watches Ava's first length: «Not bad for a beginner». What does it mean? | Good, with more still to learn | Only criticism, she swam badly | Perfect, she needs no more lessons | She should give up swimming
test: ★★★ «Mum's said a hundred times her sister is 4 years older than her. Mum is 38. How old is her sister?» | 42 | 34 | 138 | 104
test: ★★★ Gran «has told me a thousand times» she was born in 1950. In which year will she turn 80? | 2030 | 2050 | 1030 | 2080
test: ★★★ Jamal: «like a hundred keepy-uppies, well, 23». Next day: «I smashed it, like a million, no, 31». By how many did he beat his record? | 8 | 54 | 999,977 | 69
test: ★★★ Dad says «a touch of drizzle» about a hurricane, and Gran says «bit of a wait» at a bus stop after twenty minutes. What is different? | Dad's is a joke he expects you to see through; Gran's is a calm playing-down | Dad believes it is drizzle; Gran believes the wait was short | Both are hyperbole that makes things bigger than they really are | Dad's is understatement, and Gran's is sarcasm meaning the opposite
test: ★★★ Gran says «It's not that I don't like it, pet…» and stops. Mum says «I'm not unhappy with it». Who liked the dish more? | Mum | Gran | Both the same | Neither of them
test: ★★★ «Over 1 million visitors» and «A MILLION reasons to love summer». A friend says «both say a million, so both are hype». What is the flaw? | The same number can be measured in one and a feeling in the other | Both are measured, since a million is always a real number | Both are hype, because big numbers are always exaggeration | Neither is a number, because «million» is just a word
test: ★★★ In which sentence is «starving» NOT hyperbole? | «In the famine, people were starving for weeks» | «Mum, I'm starving, when's tea?» | «I'm starving, lunch was ages ago» | «Starving! I haven't eaten anything since morning break»
test: ★★★ «u nesh or what, it's nobbut a bit fresh» says Grandad. It's 12 degrees. Which reply shows you understood? | «I just feel the cold, Grandad, I'll keep my coat» | «I'm not ill, Grandad, I haven't got a cold» | «You're right, it's freezing, I'll stay in» | «What's a nesh? Is it a kind of coat?»
test: ★★★ Max: «I may have slightly broken the window». Lucy's neighbour: «it's nothing much». Both play things down. Which one should you NOT copy? | Max's, because he plays down his own mistake to dodge trouble | Mrs Tanaka's, because playing down a gift is always rude | Both, because playing anything down is always lying | Neither, because both are perfectly polite and kind
test: ★★★ «Could be worse», said after a 6–0 defeat, and «Not bad for a beginner», said after a first length. Which is praise? | «Not bad for a beginner» | «Could be worse» | Both of them | Neither of them is any kind of praise
test: ★★★ «Wah confirm one hour lah» about a queue of 10 people, each served in 2 minutes. About how long is the real wait? | About 20 minutes | About one hour | About 10 minutes | About two hours
test: ★★★ Leo, wrapped in a big coat on a mild day, hears «you're nesh». Ruby, in a T-shirt in the snow, hears «bit nippy out, innit». Who is dressed wrongly for the weather? | Ruby | Leo | Both | Neither
exam: It is given that a grandmother «has told me a thousand times» that she was born in 1950. Determine her age in the year 2000. | Fifty years | One thousand years | One thousand and fifty years | Nine hundred and fifty years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the remark «a touch of drizzle», delivered without expression by a parent viewing a hurricane? | Sarcastic understatement, intended as a jest | A literal description of light rain | Hyperbole, magnifying the importance of a minor shower | A meteorological forecast
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the word «starving» constitutes hyperbole in one context and a literal statement in another. | The circumstances determine whether the word exceeds or matches the facts | The word bears a different spelling in works of history | The word is invariably employed as hyperbole | The word is invariably employed literally
exam: A swimming instructor remarks «Not bad for a beginner». Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the remark. | Praise qualified by the expectation of further improvement | Unqualified condemnation of the performance | Unqualified praise implying that instruction is complete | A recommendation that the pupil discontinue swimming
